==================================================================
DAY 45 - PART B: LAB
Push to ECR and expose your app on the internet
Time: 90-120 minutes
==================================================================

STEP 1: Create the cluster
--------------------------
cd ~/k8s-journey/cloud/eks && eksctl create cluster -f cluster.yaml
(while it builds, do Steps 2-3)


STEP 2: Create an ECR repo and push your image
----------------------------------------------
ACCOUNT=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)
REGION=ap-south-1
REG=$ACCOUNT.dkr.ecr.$REGION.amazonaws.com

aws ecr create-repository --repository-name myapp --image-scanning-configuration scanOnPush=true
aws ecr get-login-password --region $REGION | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin $REG
(Podman: replace "docker" with "podman")

cd ~/k8s-journey/myapp
docker build -t $REG/myapp:v4 .
docker push $REG/myapp:v4
aws ecr describe-images --repository-name myapp --output table


STEP 3: Scan results
--------------------
aws ecr describe-image-scan-findings --repository-name myapp --image-id imageTag=v4 \
  --query 'imageScanFindings.findingSeverityCounts'


STEP 4: Deploy from ECR (no pull secret needed)
-----------------------------------------------
mkdir -p ~/k8s-journey/cloud/eks/app && cd $_
k create deploy myapp --image=$REG/myapp:v4 --replicas=3 --port=5000
k get pods -o wide
k describe pod -l app=myapp | grep -E "Image:|Pulled"


STEP 5: LoadBalancer Service (default cloud provider)
-----------------------------------------------------
vi svc-lb.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: myapp-lb
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: myapp
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 5000

k apply -f svc-lb.yaml
k get svc myapp-lb -w        # EXTERNAL-IP becomes a DNS name (1-3 min)
LB=$(k get svc myapp-lb -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].hostname}')
sleep 60 ; for i in 1 2 3 4; do curl -s http://$LB; done

Console: EC2 -> Load Balancers: find it. Note its type, the subnets
(public), listeners (80 -> NodePort), and registered targets (nodes).
  k get svc myapp-lb -o jsonpath='{.spec.ports[0].nodePort}{"\n"}'


STEP 6: Install the AWS Load Balancer Controller
------------------------------------------------
(Check the latest install guide in the AWS docs; steps below follow
the standard Helm + IAM approach.)

curl -sO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/aws-load-balancer-controller/main/docs/install/iam_policy.json
aws iam create-policy --policy-name AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy-lab --policy-document file://iam_policy.json

eksctl create iamserviceaccount --cluster lab --namespace kube-system \
  --name aws-load-balancer-controller \
  --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::$ACCOUNT:policy/AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy-lab \
  --approve

helm repo add eks https://aws.github.io/eks-charts && helm repo update
VPC=$(aws eks describe-cluster --name lab --query cluster.resourcesVpcConfig.vpcId --output text)
helm install aws-load-balancer-controller eks/aws-load-balancer-controller -n kube-system \
  --set clusterName=lab --set serviceAccount.create=false \
  --set serviceAccount.name=aws-load-balancer-controller \
  --set region=$REGION --set vpcId=$VPC
k get deploy -n kube-system aws-load-balancer-controller


STEP 7: NLB with IP targets (straight to pods)
----------------------------------------------
vi svc-nlb.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: myapp-nlb
  annotations:
    service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type: external
    service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-nlb-target-type: ip
    service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-scheme: internet-facing
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: myapp
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 5000

k apply -f svc-nlb.yaml
k get svc myapp-nlb -w
In the console, the NLB's target group lists POD IPs, not nodes.
Scale the deployment and watch targets change.


STEP 8: ALB Ingress
-------------------
vi ingress-alb.yaml

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: myapp
  annotations:
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internet-facing
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/healthcheck-path: /ready
spec:
  ingressClassName: alb
  rules:
  - http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: myapp-lb
            port:
              number: 80

k apply -f ingress-alb.yaml
k get ingress myapp -w          # ADDRESS appears after 2-4 minutes
ALB=$(k get ingress myapp -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].hostname}')
sleep 60 ; curl -s http://$ALB ; curl -s http://$ALB/ready


STEP 9: CLEAN UP (ORDER MATTERS)
--------------------------------
k delete ingress myapp
k delete svc myapp-lb myapp-nlb
sleep 90                                   # let controllers delete LBs
aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers --query 'LoadBalancers[].LoadBalancerName'
aws elb describe-load-balancers --query 'LoadBalancerDescriptions[].LoadBalancerName'
helm uninstall aws-load-balancer-controller -n kube-system
eksctl delete cluster -f ~/k8s-journey/cloud/eks/cluster.yaml --wait
(Keep the ECR repo; storage is cheap. The IAM policy can stay too.)


STEP 10: AKS / GKE equivalents
------------------------------
AKS: az acr create -g lab-rg -n <unique> --sku Basic ;
     az aks update -g lab-rg -n lab --attach-acr <unique> ;
     az acr login -n <unique> ; Service type LoadBalancer -> Azure LB
GKE: gcloud artifacts repositories create myapp --repository-format=docker
